{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 25, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli reigned from 1155 to 1194 a significant figure in the Crusader states particularly in Antioch and Tripoli. He was a skilled military leader a shrewd politician and a patron of the arts and religious institutions. His reign was marked by both successes and challenges navigating complex alliances and constant threats from Muslim powers.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1110 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1155. His early years were spent consolidating his power dealing with internal dissent and defending Tripoli's borders against various enemies including the Zengids. He faced challenges from rebellious vassals and had to assert his authority to maintain stability within his county. Raymond’s father had established a strong foundation for the county and Raymond worked diligently to preserve and expand it. He also fostered relationships with other Crusader rulers seeking mutual support against common foes.\n\nRaymond III's reign witnessed significant military campaigns. He engaged in numerous battles against Nur ad-Din and later Saladin striving to maintain control over Tripoli and surrounding territories. He formed alliances with other Crusader states like the Kingdom of Jerusalem and the Principality of Antioch to bolster their collective defense. His military strategies often involved a combination of defensive fortifications and opportunistic raids against enemy strongholds.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond III was a master of diplomacy. He skillfully navigated the treacherous political landscape of the Levant forging alliances with various powers to advance his interests. He maintained relations with the Byzantine Empire the Papacy and various Muslim rulers. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in 